---
// Lesson 1.5a — Movement II: Speed, deltaTime & Tuning
// The Inspector as tuning surface, serialized-vs-code precedence, feel.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.5a — Movement II: Speed, deltaTime &amp; Tuning</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The movement works; today you make it <em>feel</em> right. That means
    understanding where speed actually lives — the code, or the Inspector
    — and why deltaTime is the difference between a game and a slideshow.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Read the top of PlayerController.cs again:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float xSpeed = 5.0f;
[SerializeField] private float ySpeed = 5.0f;</code></pre>

    <p>Predict: you press Play, change xSpeed in the Inspector to 12,
    stop, and open the file. What number is in the code now? Then you
    close Play, set it to 12 <em>in edit mode</em>, and open the file
    again — what is in the code now, and what is in the Inspector?</p>

    <p>The two answers are different, and the difference is the whole
    point of [SerializeField]: the code holds a <strong>default</strong>;
    the scene/prefab holds an <strong>override</strong> that beats the
    default. The Inspector is data saved in your scene file, not in your
    .cs file.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Your fork of the starter has xSpeed = 5 in the code. If your
            friend clones your repo and opens the Overworld, do they see
            your Inspector override or the code default — and why does
            git never see the Inspector value at all?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Feel pass, one value at a time.</strong> Designers do not
    guess good numbers, they tune them — one variable, one playtest,
    repeat:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Set xSpeed to 3. Walk across the Overworld. Too slow? Probably.</li>
        <li>Try 5, then 8. Fill in a mini-table as you go: speed / how it feels / one adjective.</li>
        <li>Pick the winner in edit mode, so it saves. Do the same for ySpeed — should it match xSpeed, or is the overworld nicer slightly slower on the vertical? Decide, do not default.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Why the Inspector and not the code?</strong> Because the
        point of a tuning knob is turning it while the game runs. If you
        had to stop, edit code, wait for compile, and re-press play for
        every number, you would tune three values a day. This way you
        tune thirty.
    </div>

    <p><strong>Now break deltaTime on purpose.</strong> In
    PlayerController.Update, temporarily delete
    <code>* Time.deltaTime</code> from the xVector line. Play on this
    machine; then run the same build on a different machine if you can.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The frame-rate bug</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>With deltaTime removed, about how many times per second
                does Update run on this machine? (Your PlayerLogger from
                1.3a can count it.)</li>
            <li>If Update ran 240 times a second on a friend's gaming
                monitor, how much faster would the player be — and what
                does that do to "fair" in a game?</li>
            <li>Put deltaTime back. Where does the per-frame speed get
                divided down to per-second, exactly?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Diagonal, again, for real</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Set xSpeed and ySpeed to 8 and walk in a circle. Now hold
                A + W together: is the diagonal faster than the straight
                lines? Time it across the screen both ways.</li>
            <li>The fix is normalization — dividing the vector by its
                length so the diagonal matches. The starter does not do
                it for the player. Where WILL you meet normalization
                this semester — which NPC moves toward you at a constant
                speed no matter the angle?</li>
            <li>Change the code default of xSpeed to your tuned winner
                number and commit. Which file did you actually change —
                and does the Inspector override still beat it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain Inspector override vs code default and where each is stored</li>
            <li>Tune a speed value in play mode, then persist it in edit mode</li>
            <li>Explain what Time.deltaTime does and demonstrate the bug without it</li>
            <li>Say what normalization is for, even though you have not written it yet</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — input, vectors, deltaTime in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — the API tables</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="1.6a.html">1.6a — Animation States</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="1.5a" title="1.5a Movement II: Speed, deltaTime &amp; Tuning — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
